# PART 3 — DESIGN THE UNIFIED EDITING TRANSACTION SYSTEM

## 7. Create one authoritative editing transaction pipeline

All supported document modifications must use a consistent transaction boundary.

The intended architecture:

User action
→ Structural or property command
→ Capability and lock validation
→ Immutable PageDocument transformation
→ Working document update
→ History transaction recording
→ Canvas and inspector update
→ Dirty-state update
→ Autosave scheduling.

Reuse the command architecture from Prompts 11 and 12.

Do not allow individual UI controls to bypass the history system.

## 8. Define editing transaction metadata

An editing transaction should identify:

- Unique transaction ID.
- Action type.
- Human-readable description.
- Affected node IDs.
- Previous and next document state or reversible changes.
- Transaction creation time.
- Grouping/coalescing identity where appropriate.
- Originating editor action.
- Whether the transaction was committed.
- Whether the transaction is undoable.

Do not store passwords, Supabase credentials, or unrelated customer data.

Choose an efficient history representation after evaluating the actual document and update utilities.

## 9. Preserve immutable state

Never mutate the existing PageDocument in place.

History entries must represent stable states.

Previous history must not change when the current document is edited.

Avoid unnecessary deep clones.

Use structural sharing, patches, inverse operations, or bounded snapshots where technically appropriate.

Document and test the chosen history model.

