# PART 4 — IMPLEMENT UNDO

## 10. Implement global Undo

Provide a working Undo action.

Undo must restore the previous valid design state.

Examples:

- Change background color → Undo restores previous color.
- Increase card radius → Undo restores previous radius.
- Reorder a section → Undo restores original position.
- Delete a section → Undo restores the complete section.
- Duplicate a component → Undo removes the duplicate.
- Rename a layer → Undo restores its previous name.
- Hide a component → Undo restores visibility.
- Change mobile padding → Undo restores previous mobile padding.

Undo must operate against the validated working document, not by manually manipulating the rendered DOM.

## 11. Preserve stable identities

Undo should preserve existing node identities.

When restoring a deleted section, restore its original valid node and descendant IDs where possible.

When undoing duplication, do not regenerate unrelated IDs.

When undoing reordering, restore the original structural relationships.

Avoid unnecessary remounts of unaffected React components.

## 12. Handle invalid history safely

If a history entry cannot be applied because the document or schema has changed unexpectedly:

- Do not corrupt the current document.
- Return a structured error.
- Preserve the last valid working state.
- Provide understandable feedback.
- Record diagnostic information.

Do not silently apply an invalid patch.

