# PART 12 — HANDLE ASYNCHRONOUS SAVE RACES

## 38. Prevent stale responses from clearing dirty state

Example:

1. Administrator edits draft generation 10.
2. Autosave for generation 10 begins.
3. Administrator edits again, creating generation 11.
4. The server confirms generation 10.

The UI must not display generation 11 as saved.

Generation 11 is still unsaved.

Update persisted revision metadata appropriately and schedule the newer save.

## 39. Handle out-of-order responses

Network operations may complete in an unexpected order.

Use a disciplined save coordinator.

Prefer a single in-flight save per page/draft where appropriate, with coalescing of newer pending edits.

Do not allow two uncontrolled writes to overwrite each other.

## 40. Prevent stale server data from overwriting local changes

Refetching the draft must not automatically replace a newer local working document.

Explicitly differentiate:

- Initial draft load.
- Background refresh.
- Save acknowledgement.
- Conflict response.
- Intentional user reload.

Do not overwrite unsaved changes during background query refetching.

