# PART 15 — RECOVERY AFTER REFRESH OR BROWSER RESTART

## 48. Recover successfully persisted drafts

The primary recovery mechanism must be the secure Supabase draft.

After reopening the Design Studio:

- Authenticate the administrator.
- Load the correct page draft.
- Validate the stored PageDocument.
- Restore its saved design configuration.
- Restore the server revision.
- Render the actual canvas.
- Show the correct saved status.

Do not load an unrelated store's draft.

## 49. Evaluate recovery of unsaved local work

Some work may not yet have reached the server when the browser closes.

Design a safe optional recovery mechanism.

Possible approaches include:

- Session-scoped recovery.
- IndexedDB-based temporary checkpoints.
- Other appropriately protected browser-local storage.

Evaluate privacy and security implications before storing unpublished design documents locally.

### Required security considerations

- Scope recovery to the appropriate user, store, page, and draft.
- Never store authentication tokens alongside recovery data.
- Avoid storing secrets or private credentials.
- Define retention limits.
- Remove recovery data appropriately after confirmed persistence, logout, or expiration.
- Do not restore data into a different user's session.
- Recognize that client-side encryption with a key accessible to the same application is not a complete protection against XSS.

Do not claim secure offline recovery merely because the document was written to localStorage.

If safe local recovery cannot be implemented within the existing threat model, prioritize verified server draft recovery and clearly report the limitation.

## 50. Make recovery explicit

If a newer unsaved local recovery checkpoint exists:

- Identify its page/draft scope.
- Compare it with the server revision.
- Show an understandable recovery choice.
- Avoid silently overwriting the saved server draft.
- Allow the administrator to discard stale recovery data.

Do not automatically replay an old recovery checkpoint over a newer published or server-saved design.

